\title{mediaPlanR: Reliable Cross-Media Reach and Frequency Planning}
\description{
Reproducible tools for cross-media reach, contact-frequency distributions,
target-audience metrics and budget-constrained reach optimization. The
package combines a validated media-plan data contract with the classical
reach and exposure-distribution models of the media-planning literature.
}
\section{Scope of the validation}{

The models can be applied to any mix of media, but the published evidence on
their accuracy comes mainly from magazine and Internet data. Their accuracy
for a particular combination of media and population should be checked
against observed data, for example with \code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}}.
}

\section{Notation and units}{

The same quantity has the same meaning in every function. Names follow the
sources of each model only where the literature fixes them.
\itemize{
\item \emph{Population} (number of people in the planning universe): \code{population}
everywhere. In \code{\link[=audience_metrics]{audience_metrics()}}, \code{gross_universe} is the whole
population and \code{target_universe} the people who belong to the target.
\item \emph{Audience} (people exposed by one insertion of a vehicle): \code{audience}
in \code{\link[=media_plan]{media_plan()}} and \code{audiences} (one value per vehicle) in the
\verb{calc_*()} functions, always in people. As a proportion of the
population it is \code{R1}.
\item \emph{Reach after the first and second insertion of the same vehicle}:
\code{R1} and \code{R2} (Kim, 2005, and the sources of each model), always as
proportions of the population, in \code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}},
\code{\link[=calc_hofmans_accumulation]{calc_hofmans_accumulation()}}, \code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}} and the
sequential models. \code{R2} is cumulative: the people exposed at least once
in two insertions. If the audience is known in people, divide it by the
population.
\item \emph{Number of insertions}: \code{insertions} (one value per vehicle) in all
functions; the formulas use \eqn{n_i} for a vehicle and \eqn{N} for the
total. The column of \code{vehicles_data} in \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}} may also be named
\code{k}, Danaher's notation. The argument \code{k} of
\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}} is something else: Agostini's duplication
coefficient.
\item \emph{Duplication between two vehicles}: \code{duplication_matrix}, in people
(\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}}, \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}},
\code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}}); \code{duplications}, as proportions of the
population (\code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}} and the sequential models). The different
names signal the different units.
\item \emph{Reach} (also net audience) counts each person once;
\emph{gross audience} (also duplicated audience) is the sum of the audiences
and counts a person once per audience, as in impressions.
\item \emph{Effective frequency}: \code{effective_frequency} in
\code{\link[=optimize_media_plan]{optimize_media_plan()}} and \code{frequency} in \code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}; effective
