\section{Assumptions of the models}{

Each model rests on assumptions about four things. They are the first thing
to check when choosing a model.
\itemize{
\item \emph{Individuals}: homogeneous (every person has the same probability of
being exposed to an insertion of a vehicle) or heterogeneous
(probabilities differ between people, as between loyal and occasional
readers). Heterogeneity makes repeated exposures concentrate in the same
people, so reach grows more slowly with the number of insertions.
\code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} assume homogeneous individuals;
the Beta-Binomial family (\code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}}, \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}},
\code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}, \code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}}, \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}},
\code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}) lets the probability vary between people.
\item \emph{Vehicles}: homogeneous (every vehicle has the plan's average
audience) or heterogeneous (each has its own audience).
\code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}} treat vehicles as
homogeneous; \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} and the multivariate models keep each
vehicle's audience.
\item \emph{Duplication between vehicles}: random (people choose vehicles
independently, so the audience shared by two vehicles is the product of
their audiences) or observed (taken from data). \code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and
\code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} assume random duplication; the other models with
several vehicles use observed duplication.
\item \emph{Accumulation in the same vehicle}: random (a repeat insertion in
a vehicle is as independent as an insertion in another vehicle) or
observed through the reach after two insertions (\code{R2}), which captures
that those who saw the first insertion tend to see the next.
\code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} assume random accumulation; the
models for several insertions in a vehicle use the observed
accumulation: \code{R2} or, in \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}}, the audience
duplicated between two insertions of the same vehicle.
}
All the models also assume that exposure probabilities are stationary
(they do not change over the period of the plan). Each help page states the
assumptions of its model.
}

\section{Planning workflow}{

\code{\link[=media_plan]{media_plan()}} creates a validated plan, \code{\link[=plan_metrics]{plan_metrics()}} and
\code{\link[=audience_metrics]{audience_metrics()}} compute its metrics, \code{\link[=estimate_reach]{estimate_reach()}} and
\code{\link[=compare_reach_models]{compare_reach_models()}} estimate reach and the exposure distribution, and
\code{\link[=optimize_media_plan]{optimize_media_plan()}} allocates insertions under a budget.
}

\section{Reach and exposure-distribution models}{

Plans with one or more insertions in each of several vehicles, with random
duplication and accumulation: \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}}, \code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}}.
One vehicle with several insertions (accumulation models):
\code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}}, \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_accumulation]{calc_hofmans_accumulation()}}. Several vehicles with one insertion each:
\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}}, \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}}. Several
vehicles with several insertions: \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}}, \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}},
\code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}}, \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}} and \code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}. Fitting and
validation: \code{\link[=fit_bbd_to_reach]{fit_bbd_to_reach()}}, \code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}, \code{\link[=fit_nbd_exposure]{fit_nbd_exposure()}},
\code{\link[=nbd_exposure_distribution]{nbd_exposure_distribution()}} and \code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}}.
}

